{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108",
  "title": "Double Pendulum",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": null,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Double Pendulum \u2014 Chaos in Motion \ud83c\udfaf\n\nThe **double pendulum** is one of the simplest physical systems that exhibits **chaotic behavior**. Two rigid rods connected end-to-end, swinging under gravity \u2014 yet the motion is wildly unpredictable.\n\n## Lagrangian Mechanics\n\nInstead of tracking forces, we use the **Lagrangian** L = T - V (kinetic minus potential energy) and derive equations of motion from:\n\n> **d/dt (\u2202L/\u2202\u03b8\u0307) - \u2202L/\u2202\u03b8 = 0**\n\nFor a double pendulum with masses m\u2081, m\u2082 and rod lengths L\u2081, L\u2082, the resulting equations are coupled, nonlinear, and **have no closed-form solution** \u2014 making numerical simulation essential.\n\n## Parameters\n\n- Masses: m\u2081 = m\u2082 = 1 kg\n- Lengths: L\u2081 = L\u2082 = 1 m\n- Gravity: g = 9.81 m/s\u00b2\n- Initial angles: \u03b8\u2081 = \u03b8\u2082 = \u03c0/2 (horizontal)\n- Integration: **4th-order Runge-Kutta** (RK4)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "RK4 Simulation",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Double pendulum simulation using 4th-order Runge-Kutta integration\n\nlet m1 = 1.0_f64;\nlet m2 = 1.0_f64;\nlet l1 = 1.0_f64;\nlet l2 = 1.0_f64;\nlet g = 9.81_f64;\nlet dt = 0.01_f64;\nlet steps = 5000_usize;\n\n// State: [theta1, theta2, omega1, omega2]\nlet mut state = [\n    std::f64::consts::FRAC_PI_2, // theta1 = pi/2\n    std::f64::consts::FRAC_PI_2, // theta2 = pi/2\n    0.0_f64,                      // omega1\n    0.0_f64,                      // omega2\n];\n\n// Equations of motion for the double pendulum\nfn derivs(s: &[f64; 4], m1: f64, m2: f64, l1: f64, l2: f64, g: f64) -> [f64; 4] {\n    let [t1, t2, w1, w2] = *s;\n    let delta = t1 - t2;\n    let sin_d = delta.sin();\n    let cos_d = delta.cos();\n    let denom = 2.0 * m1 + m2 - m2 * (2.0 * delta).cos();\n\n    let dw1 = (-g * (2.0 * m1 + m2) * t1.sin()\n        - m2 * g * (t1 - 2.0 * t2).sin()\n        - 2.0 * sin_d * m2 * (w2 * w2 * l2 + w1 * w1 * l1 * cos_d))\n        / (l1 * denom);\n\n    let dw2 = (2.0 * sin_d\n        * (w1 * w1 * l1 * (m1 + m2)\n            + g * (m1 + m2) * t1.cos()\n            + w2 * w2 * l2 * m2 * cos_d))\n        / (l2 * denom);\n\n    [w1, w2, dw1, dw2]\n}\n\n// Collect the trajectory of the second pendulum tip (x2, y2)\nlet mut trajectory: Vec<(f64, f64)> = Vec::with_capacity(steps + 1);\n\n// Cartesian position of the tip\nlet x2 = l1 * state[0].sin() + l2 * state[1].sin();\nlet y2 = -(l1 * state[0].cos() + l2 * state[1].cos());\ntrajectory.push((x2, y2));\n\n// RK4 integration\nfor _ in 0..steps {\n    let k1 = derivs(&state, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n\n    let mut s2 = state;\n    for j in 0..4 { s2[j] += 0.5 * dt * k1[j]; }\n    let k2 = derivs(&s2, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n\n    let mut s3 = state;\n    for j in 0..4 { s3[j] += 0.5 * dt * k2[j]; }\n    let k3 = derivs(&s3, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n\n    let mut s4 = state;\n    for j in 0..4 { s4[j] += dt * k3[j]; }\n    let k4 = derivs(&s4, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n\n    for j in 0..4 {\n        state[j] += (dt / 6.0) * (k1[j] + 2.0 * k2[j] + 2.0 * k3[j] + k4[j]);\n    }\n\n    let x2 = l1 * state[0].sin() + l2 * state[1].sin();\n    let y2 = -(l1 * state[0].cos() + l2 * state[1].cos());\n    trajectory.push((x2, y2));\n}\n\n// Return sampled trajectory (every 5th point) for piping\nlet sampled: Vec<(f64, f64)> = trajectory.iter()\n    .step_by(5)\n    .copied()\n    .collect();\n\nsampled\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_1\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Trajectory Visualization",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\n// cell1 contains the sampled (x2, y2) trajectory of the pendulum tip\nlet trajectory: &Vec<(f64, f64)> = &cell1;\nlet n = trajectory.len();\n\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (700, 700))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(15, 15, 25)).unwrap();\n\n    let mut chart = ChartBuilder::on(&root)\n        .caption(\"Double Pendulum Tip Trajectory\", (\"sans-serif\", 22).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .margin(15)\n        .x_label_area_size(35)\n        .y_label_area_size(45)\n        .build_cartesian_2d(-2.5_f64..2.5, -2.5_f64..2.5)\n        .unwrap();\n\n    chart.configure_mesh()\n        .label_style((\"sans-serif\", 12).into_font().color(&RGBColor(180, 180, 180)))\n        .axis_style(RGBColor(60, 60, 80))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    // Draw trajectory segments, color-coded from blue (early) to red (late)\n    for i in 0..(n - 1) {\n        let t = i as f64 / (n - 1) as f64;\n        // Blue -> Cyan -> Green -> Yellow -> Red\n        let r = (255.0 * (2.0 * t).min(1.0)) as u8;\n        let g_c = (255.0 * (1.0 - (2.0 * t - 1.0).abs())) as u8;\n        let b = (255.0 * (1.0 - 2.0 * t).max(0.0)) as u8;\n        let color = RGBColor(r, g_c, b);\n\n        chart.draw_series(LineSeries::new(\n            [trajectory[i], trajectory[i + 1]].iter().copied(),\n            color,\n        ))\n        .unwrap();\n    }\n\n    // Mark the start and end points\n    if let (Some(&start), Some(&end)) = (trajectory.first(), trajectory.last()) {\n        chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Circle::new(start, 6, RGBColor(80, 130, 255).filled()),\n        )).unwrap();\n        chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Circle::new(end, 6, RGBColor(255, 80, 80).filled()),\n        )).unwrap();\n    }\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_2\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Sensitivity to Initial Conditions",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\n// Simulate TWO double pendulums with slightly different initial theta1\n// to demonstrate sensitivity to initial conditions (chaos)\n\nlet m1 = 1.0_f64;\nlet m2 = 1.0_f64;\nlet l1 = 1.0_f64;\nlet l2 = 1.0_f64;\nlet g = 9.81_f64;\nlet dt = 0.01_f64;\nlet steps = 5000_usize;\n\nfn derivs(s: &[f64; 4], m1: f64, m2: f64, l1: f64, l2: f64, g: f64) -> [f64; 4] {\n    let [t1, t2, w1, w2] = *s;\n    let delta = t1 - t2;\n    let sin_d = delta.sin();\n    let cos_d = delta.cos();\n    let denom = 2.0 * m1 + m2 - m2 * (2.0 * delta).cos();\n\n    let dw1 = (-g * (2.0 * m1 + m2) * t1.sin()\n        - m2 * g * (t1 - 2.0 * t2).sin()\n        - 2.0 * sin_d * m2 * (w2 * w2 * l2 + w1 * w1 * l1 * cos_d))\n        / (l1 * denom);\n    let dw2 = (2.0 * sin_d\n        * (w1 * w1 * l1 * (m1 + m2)\n            + g * (m1 + m2) * t1.cos()\n            + w2 * w2 * l2 * m2 * cos_d))\n        / (l2 * denom);\n\n    [w1, w2, dw1, dw2]\n}\n\nfn simulate(\n    theta1: f64, theta2: f64,\n    m1: f64, m2: f64, l1: f64, l2: f64, g: f64,\n    dt: f64, steps: usize,\n) -> Vec<(f64, f64)> {\n    let mut state = [theta1, theta2, 0.0, 0.0];\n    let mut traj = Vec::with_capacity(steps + 1);\n    traj.push((\n        l1 * state[0].sin() + l2 * state[1].sin(),\n        -(l1 * state[0].cos() + l2 * state[1].cos()),\n    ));\n\n    for _ in 0..steps {\n        let k1 = derivs(&state, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n        let mut s2 = state;\n        for j in 0..4 { s2[j] += 0.5 * dt * k1[j]; }\n        let k2 = derivs(&s2, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n        let mut s3 = state;\n        for j in 0..4 { s3[j] += 0.5 * dt * k2[j]; }\n        let k3 = derivs(&s3, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n        let mut s4 = state;\n        for j in 0..4 { s4[j] += dt * k3[j]; }\n        let k4 = derivs(&s4, m1, m2, l1, l2, g);\n        for j in 0..4 {\n            state[j] += (dt / 6.0) * (k1[j] + 2.0 * k2[j] + 2.0 * k3[j] + k4[j]);\n        }\n        traj.push((\n            l1 * state[0].sin() + l2 * state[1].sin(),\n            -(l1 * state[0].cos() + l2 * state[1].cos()),\n        ));\n    }\n    traj\n}\n\nlet pi_2 = std::f64::consts::FRAC_PI_2;\n\n// Pendulum A: original initial conditions\nlet traj_a = simulate(pi_2, pi_2, m1, m2, l1, l2, g, dt, steps);\n\n// Pendulum B: theta1 differs by only 0.01 radians (~0.57 degrees)\nlet traj_b = simulate(pi_2 + 0.01, pi_2, m1, m2, l1, l2, g, dt, steps);\n\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (700, 700))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(15, 15, 25)).unwrap();\n\n    let mut chart = ChartBuilder::on(&root)\n        .caption(\n            \"Two Pendulums: \\u{0394}\\u{03B8}\\u{2081} = 0.01 rad\",\n            (\"sans-serif\", 22).into_font().color(&WHITE),\n        )\n        .margin(15)\n        .x_label_area_size(35)\n        .y_label_area_size(45)\n        .build_cartesian_2d(-2.5_f64..2.5, -2.5_f64..2.5)\n        .unwrap();\n\n    chart.configure_mesh()\n        .label_style((\"sans-serif\", 12).into_font().color(&RGBColor(180, 180, 180)))\n        .axis_style(RGBColor(60, 60, 80))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    // Draw Pendulum A trail (cyan)\n    chart.draw_series(LineSeries::new(\n        traj_a.iter().step_by(2).copied(),\n        RGBColor(80, 200, 255),\n    ))\n    .unwrap()\n    .label(\"Pendulum A\")\n    .legend(|(lx, ly)| {\n        Rectangle::new([(lx, ly - 4), (lx + 12, ly + 4)], RGBColor(80, 200, 255).filled())\n    });\n\n    // Draw Pendulum B trail (orange)\n    chart.draw_series(LineSeries::new(\n        traj_b.iter().step_by(2).copied(),\n        RGBColor(255, 160, 50),\n    ))\n    .unwrap()\n    .label(\"Pendulum B (+0.01 rad)\")\n    .legend(|(lx, ly)| {\n        Rectangle::new([(lx, ly - 4), (lx + 12, ly + 4)], RGBColor(255, 160, 50).filled())\n    });\n\n    chart\n        .configure_series_labels()\n        .background_style(RGBColor(30, 30, 45).mix(0.9))\n        .label_font((\"sans-serif\", 13).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .border_style(RGBColor(80, 80, 100))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_3\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_4",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "The Butterfly Effect",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The Butterfly Effect \ud83e\udd8b\n\nThe two pendulums above started with angles differing by only **0.01 radians** (about 0.57\u00b0). At first their paths are nearly identical \u2014 but they **rapidly diverge** into completely different trajectories.\n\nThis is the hallmark of **deterministic chaos**:\n\n1. **Deterministic**: Given exact initial conditions, the future is completely determined.\n2. **Sensitive**: Infinitesimal changes in initial conditions lead to exponentially diverging outcomes.\n3. **Bounded**: The system stays within physical limits despite the unpredictability.\n\n### Lyapunov Exponent\n\nThe rate of divergence is measured by the **Lyapunov exponent** \u03bb. Two nearby trajectories separate as:\n\n> **|\u03b4(t)| \u2248 |\u03b4\u2080| \u00b7 e^(\u03bbt)**\n\nA positive \u03bb means chaos. The double pendulum has a positive maximum Lyapunov exponent, making long-term prediction fundamentally impossible without infinite precision.\n\n### Why It Matters\n\nThis same sensitivity appears in weather systems (Lorenz's \"butterfly flapping its wings\"), turbulent fluids, population dynamics, and even the long-term stability of planetary orbits.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ]
}
